**So, it turns out we can work logic into coding**

For this assignment, I was tasked with writing a piece of code that took in two boolean variables (variables that are either true or false, so I would
receive true and true or false and true) then making a different light light up on an arduino board for each combination. Now, as there are 4 different combinations
of true or false that could be inputted, I needed to find a way to express 4 different cases in my code. I chose nested if statements, like : If my first variable 
is true, then if the second one is turn on light 1, and if the second one is not, turn on light two. Otherwise, if my second variable is true, turn on light three, 
and if the second is also false, turn on light 4. I see if statements as a way to introduce a new possibility, so I ended up needing 4 if statements for 4 
possible outcomes, which made sense to me. That was it. I do wish I used autoformat sooner when I was writing my code, because that made it so much easier to read 
while I was working. 
